In other words hAi is the smallest subgroup of G that contains A. We will prove the following theorem. Theorem 1. Let G = A + B be a normalized factorization of the finite cyclic group G. If |A| = q is a prime, hAi = G, then B is a subgroup of G. The message in Theorem 1 is that in a class of splitting problems we may focus our 7 attention to coset splittings. For the details we need two lemmas. Lemma 1. Let G = A + B be a factorization of the abelian group G and let H = hAi. Then H = A + (B ∩ H) is a factorization H. Proof. Choose a h ∈ H. Since h ∈ G and G = A + B is a factorization of G it follows that h can be represented uniquely in the form h = a + b, a ∈ A, b ∈ B. From b = h − a, h ∈ H, a ∈ H we get that b ∈ H and so b ∈ B ∩ H. Therefore each h can be represented uniquely in the form h = a + b, a ∈ A, b ∈ B ∩ H which completes the proof. We will refer to the result in Lemma 1 saying that the factorization G = A + B can be restricted to H to get the factorization H = A + (B ∩ H).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProof of a conjecture of Stanley about Stern's array - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Proof_of_a_conjecture_of_Stanley_about_Stern%27s_array)\nciteturn10search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Proof of a conjecture of Stanley about Stern's array ... Abstract: Stanley, building on work of Stern, defined an array of numbers by the recurrence {\\displaystyle s(n,2k)=s(n-1,k)}, {\\displaystyle s(n,2k+1)=s(n-1,k)+s(n-1,k+1)}. ...   * Stanley's conjectures on the Stern poset\nNumerical evidence, however, suggested that {\\displaystyle {s}_{n}^{r}} obeys shorter recurrences, of length {\\displaystyle r/3+O(1)}. We prove Stanley's conjecture.\n\nRecommendations\n\n  * Stanley's conjectures on the Stern poset\n  * Proof of a conjecture of Stanley-Zanello\n  * Proof of Stahl's conjecture in some new cases\n  * A proof of Steutel's conjecture\n  * On a Conjecture